QB.add(44,[{"n":1,"q":"A 58-year-old male presents to the clinic with significant fatigue, lymphadenopathy, and unintentional weight loss over the past few months. He has a history of HIV and is currently on antiretroviral therapy. A biopsy of the enlarged lymph nodes reveals aggressive B-cell neoplasm. Which of the following types of non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma is most likely associated with his condition?","o":[{"t":"Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma"},{"t":"Gastric MALT lymphoma"},{"t":"Multicentric Castleman’s disease"},{"t":"Primary CNS diffuse large B-cell lymphoma"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical scenario mentions both the presence of an HIV infection and a non-Hodgkin’s B-cell type lymphoma, which points to the diffuse large B-cell lymphoma that is associated with HIV patients."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Types of non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma based on infectious agents:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-Hodgkin’s lymphomas can be associated with infectious agents."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Infectious agent Lymphoid malignancy"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Epstein-Barr virus Burkitt’s lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Post-organ transplant lymphoma Primary CNS diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (Option D ruled out) Hodgkin’s lymphoma Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"HTLV-1 Adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"HIV Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Burkitt’s lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hepatitis C virus Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Helicobacter pylori Gastric MALT lymphoma (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Human herpes virus 8 Primary effusion lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Multicentric Castleman’s disease (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Key points:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HTLV-1 has a long latency from infection to oncogenesis as it causes adult T-cell leukaemia in people who were infected via an infected mother’s breast milk as babies, with the mean age of presentation being 56 years."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In HIV-infected patients, aggressive B-cell NHL develops."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Grading of Non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The grade of the lymphoma is a significant factor clinically as it determines the rate of proliferation of the disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High-grade NHL Low-grade NHL"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This type has a high rate of proliferation. It has a low proliferation rate. Rapidly produces symptoms. It may stay asymptomatic for months or years. It is fatal if not treated. However, it is curable. It has a slow course of disease."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"It is not curable with conventional treatments."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Over n rds of the NHL in the world are either Diffuse large B-cell NHL, which is high-grade NHL or Follicular NHL, which is a low-grade NHL."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 35-year-old male presents with cervical lymphadenopathy, fever, night sweats, and unintentional weight loss. A lymph node biopsy reveals cells, as shown in the image below. Which of the following statements is consistent with this diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Bone marrow involvement is often present."},{"t":"Frequently associated with Epstein-Barr virus"},{"t":"Gastrointestinal involvement is commonly observed"},{"t":"Associated with non-contiguous lymph nodes spread."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Frequently associated with Epstein-Barr virus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cervical lymphadenopathy with fever, night sweats, and weight loss (B symptoms), along with the presence of characteristic Reed-Sternberg cells, points towards a diagnosis of Hodgkin Lymphoma (HL). HL is commonly associated with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Feature Hodgkin Lymphoma (HL) Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma (NHL)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incidence 4 new cases per 1 lakh population 12 new cases per 1 lakh population"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"per year per year"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Age Median age 31 years; Median age 65-70 years"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"First peak at 20-35 years and second at 50-70 years"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Presentation Contiguous lymph node enlargement Non-contiguous spread (Option D)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Stage at diagnosis Mostly localized Commonly widespread"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sites Nodal; supradiaphragmatic Nodal or extranodal; any site"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) Frequently (Option B) Less commonly involvement"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cellular Characteristics Reed-Sternberg cells present No Reed-Sternberg cells; various"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"histological types"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Feature Hodgkin Lymphoma (HL) Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma (NHL)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Constitutional symptoms Alter prognosis Do not affect prognosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Central nervous system Rare Occurrence increases with AIDS. involvement"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mediastinal involvement Most common with nodular Most common with lymphoblastic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"sclerosing HL lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gastrointestinal involvement Rare Occurs (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Abdominal nodal Can be small or large, mesenteric Usually enlarged, mesenteric involvement rare common"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bone involvement Rare Occurs"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Marrow involvement Rare Common (Option A)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Prognosis 70-80% cure Highly variable by type; mostly"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"incurable"}],"qi":[6900]},{"n":3,"q":"A 50-year-old patient presents with chronic eczematous lesions on his thighs for 6 years. He had visited a dermatologist previously, and a biopsy of the lesion and histopathological examination was done as treatment was not effective and he was referred to the physician. The HPE report shows Pautrier microabscesses. What is the most probable diagnosis in this case?","o":[{"t":"Sezary syndrome"},{"t":"Adult T-cell lymphoma"},{"t":"Subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma"},{"t":"Mycosis fungoides"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Mycosis fungoides"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical features of eczematous lesions with Pautrier microabscesses on histopathology point to diagnosing mycosis fungoides , the most common cutaneous T-cell lymphoma ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"WHO types of T-cell varieties of non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma are as follows:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"T-cell granular lymphocytic leukemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma (HTLV-1+)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mycosis fungoides"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sezary syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral T-cell lymphoma NOS"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Angioimmunoblastic T-cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anaplastic large-cell lymphoma, ALK+"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anaplastic large-cell lymphoma, ALK–"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cutaneous T-cell lymphomas:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The mature T-cell disorders include cutaneous lymphomas and peripheral T-cell lymphomas."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mycosis fungoides Sezary syndrome (Option A)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This is the most common cutaneous T-cell lymphoma This condition slowly develops from mycosis that mimics benign skin diseases. fungoides and is an aggressive form."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It usually presents with chronic eczematous lesions . It is an erythrodermic cutaneous T-cell"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"lymphoma with a leukemic component."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The skin lesions are thin erythematous plaques or flat Skin lesions are intense , widespread pruritic patches . erythroderma . They are single or multiple lesions in the gluteal It involves ~80% of the total body surface area. region/thighs."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"On histopathology, atypical cells are seen as Pautrier microabscesses may or may not be aggregates in the epidermal layer, forming Pautrier present. microabscesses . Sparse dermal infiltrate is seen and may lack Epidermotropism of atypical cells is seen. epidermotropism."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"The atypical circulating lymphocytes are called Sezary cells having cerebriform nuclei ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The treatment goal is mainly palliative, with the usage of topical/systemic steroids for pruritis and other symptoms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mycosis fungoides Sezary syndrome (Option A)"},{"k":"img","v":6924},{"k":"img","v":6929},{"k":"img","v":6934},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Adult T-cell lymphoma (Option B) : It is associated with the HTLV-1 virus and contracted via infected breast milk. It typically presents at around age 60 with circulating disease, bone marrow involvement, hypercalcemia, lytic bone lesions, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, skin lesions, and opportunistic infections."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma (Option C) is a rare condition often mistaken for panniculitis. It presents in young men in their fifth and sixth decades of life with multiple subcutaneous nodules that may progress to ulceration."}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 12-year-old male presents with a rapidly enlarging jaw mass and facial swelling. Imaging reveals extensive extranodal involvement. Biopsy shows a starry sky appearance, and immunohistochemistry is positive for CD19, CD20, and CD10 but negative for BCL2. EBV testing is positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Burkitt's Lymphoma"},{"t":"Follicular Lymphoma"},{"t":"Mantle Cell Lymphoma"},{"t":"Diffuse Large B-cell lymphoma"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Burkitt's Lymphoma"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Rapidly enlarging jaw mass, \"starry sky\" biopsy appearance, positive CD19/CD20/CD10 markers, and t(8;14) translocation, along with EBV positivity, point to Burkitt's lymphoma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Burkitt's Lymphoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Constitutes ~30% of childhood NHL."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fastest-growing neoplasms with a doubling time of <24 hours."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Predominantly affects males."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically seen in patients <35 years of age."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Associated with a translocation involving C-MYC on chromosome 8: t(8;14): M/c, t(8;2), t(8,22)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Types and Presentations:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Endemic (African) form: Presents as a jaw or facial bone tumour, spreading to extranodal sites such as ovaries, testis, kidneys, breasts, bone marrow, and meninges. Jaw involvement is characteristic of endemic Burkitt’s lymphoma. EBV positive."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presents as a jaw or facial bone tumour, spreading to extranodal sites such as ovaries, testis, kidneys, breasts, bone marrow, and meninges."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Jaw involvement is characteristic of endemic Burkitt’s lymphoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"EBV positive."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presents as a jaw or facial bone tumour, spreading to extranodal sites such as ovaries, testis, kidneys, breasts, bone marrow, and meninges."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Jaw involvement is characteristic of endemic Burkitt’s lymphoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"EBV positive."},{"k":"img","v":6941},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-endemic form: Has an abdominal presentation with massive disease, ascites, and involvement of organs like the kidneys, testis, and ovaries, also spreading to the bone marrow and CNS. EBV negative."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Has an abdominal presentation with massive disease, ascites, and involvement of organs like the kidneys, testis, and ovaries, also spreading to the bone marrow and CNS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"EBV negative."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunodeficiency-related form: More often involves lymph nodes and may present as acute leukaemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Has an abdominal presentation with massive disease, ascites, and involvement of organs like the kidneys, testis, and ovaries, also spreading to the bone marrow and CNS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"EBV negative."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Histopathology: High proliferation rate with \" starry sky \" appearance due to tingible body macrophages ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High proliferation rate with \" starry sky \" appearance due to tingible body macrophages ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High proliferation rate with \" starry sky \" appearance due to tingible body macrophages ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunophenotype: Positive for B-cell antigens CD19, CD20, surface immunoglobulin, CD10, and BCL6. Negative for BCL2."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Positive for B-cell antigens CD19, CD20, surface immunoglobulin, CD10, and BCL6."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Negative for BCL2."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Positive for B-cell antigens CD19, CD20, surface immunoglobulin, CD10, and BCL6."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Negative for BCL2."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment: Extremely chemosensitive; early initiation of treatment is crucial due to the rapid doubling time. Prophylactic therapy to the CNS is mandatory. Dose-adjusted EPOCH-R (rituximab, etoposide, vincristine, doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide, prednisone) is highlyeffective. Salvage therapy is generally ineffective in relapsed cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extremely chemosensitive; early initiation of treatment is crucial due to the rapid doubling time."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prophylactic therapy to the CNS is mandatory."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dose-adjusted EPOCH-R (rituximab, etoposide, vincristine, doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide, prednisone) is highly effective."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Salvage therapy is generally ineffective in relapsed cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extremely chemosensitive; early initiation of treatment is crucial due to the rapid doubling time."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prophylactic therapy to the CNS is mandatory."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dose-adjusted EPOCH-R (rituximab, etoposide, vincristine, doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide, prednisone) is highly effective."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Salvage therapy is generally ineffective in relapsed cases."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Follicular Lymphoma (Option A) is a slow-growing B-cell lymphoproliferative disorder characterized by BCL2 positivity, whereas this case presents with a fast-growing mass and BCL2 negativity, ruling it out."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mantle Cell Lymphoma (Option B) typically presents with cyclin D1 overexpression and does not show the \"starry sky\" appearance or the immunophenotype (CD10 positivity) characteristic of Burkitt's Lymphoma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (Option C): While DLBCL can present with a rapidly enlarging mass, it typically does not show the \"starry sky\" appearance, which is distinctive for Burkitt's Lymphoma. DLBCL is also more commonly associated with BCL2 positivity."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 67-year-old male presents with a rapidly growing abdominal mass, fever, night sweats, and weight loss. His lab results show elevated LDH levels and a biopsy reveals large atypical lymphocytes expressing CD19 and CD20 with negative Cyclin D1. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Follicular Lymphoma"},{"t":"Mantle Cell Lymphoma"},{"t":"Classical Hodgkin's Lymphoma"},{"t":"Diffuse Large B-cell lymphoma"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Diffuse Large B-cell lymphoma"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The presence of a rapidly growing abdominal mass, systemic B symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss), elevated LDH levels, and biopsy findings showing large atypical lymphocytes expressing CD19 and CD20 point towards a diagnosis of diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (DLBCL):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Most common subtype of Non-Hodgkin's Lymphoma (NHL)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Age > 64 years"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Men > Female"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Often related to Epstein-Barr virus and HIV."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presentation: Typically presents as a rapidly growing mass or enlarging lymph nodes in nodal or extranodal sites. The majority of patients have advanced-stage disease at diagnosis. ~40% have B symptoms (fever, night sweats, and weight loss), and 50% have elevated LDH levels. Up to 40% have non-lymph node involvement: Bone marrow, CNS, GI tract, thyroid, liver, and skin. M/c primary extranodal site: Gastrointestinal tract > Skin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically presents as a rapidly growing mass or enlarging lymph nodes in nodal or extranodal sites."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The majority of patients have advanced-stage disease at diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"~40% have B symptoms (fever, night sweats, and weight loss), and 50% have elevated LDH levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Up to 40% have non-lymph node involvement: Bone marrow, CNS, GI tract, thyroid, liver, and skin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"M/c primary extranodal site: Gastrointestinal tract > Skin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically presents as a rapidly growing mass or enlarging lymph nodes in nodal or extranodal sites."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The majority of patients have advanced-stage disease at diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"~40% have B symptoms (fever, night sweats, and weight loss), and 50% have elevated LDH levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Up to 40% have non-lymph node involvement: Bone marrow, CNS, GI tract, thyroid, liver, and skin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"M/c primary extranodal site: Gastrointestinal tract > Skin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tumor Characteristics: Diffuse proliferation of large atypical lymphocytes with high proliferative index. Expression of B-cell markers: CD19, CD20, CD79a. Types: Germinal centre subtype (GCB): BCL6 and CD10 expression Non-GCB (ABC subtype): MUM1 expression. BCL2 is overexpressed in 25-80% of cases, and BCL6 positive in >2/3 of cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diffuse proliferation of large atypical lymphocytes with high proliferative index."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Expression of B-cell markers: CD19, CD20, CD79a."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Types: Germinal centre subtype (GCB): BCL6 and CD10 expression Non-GCB (ABC subtype): MUM1 expression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Germinal centre subtype (GCB): BCL6 and CD10 expression"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-GCB (ABC subtype): MUM1 expression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"BCL2 is overexpressed in 25-80% of cases, and BCL6 positive in >2/3 of cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diffuse proliferation of large atypical lymphocytes with high proliferative index."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Expression of B-cell markers: CD19, CD20, CD79a."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Types: Germinal centre subtype (GCB): BCL6 and CD10 expression Non-GCB (ABC subtype): MUM1 expression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Germinal centre subtype (GCB): BCL6 and CD10 expression"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-GCB (ABC subtype): MUM1 expression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"BCL2 is overexpressed in 25-80% of cases, and BCL6 positive in >2/3 of cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Germinal centre subtype (GCB): BCL6 and CD10 expression"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-GCB (ABC subtype): MUM1 expression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Important Terminologies: Double hit lymphoma: Involves MYC rearrangement along with BCL2 and/or BCL6. Triple hit lymphoma: Involves rearrangements in BCL2, BCL6, and MYC."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Double hit lymphoma: Involves MYC rearrangement along with BCL2 and/or BCL6."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Triple hit lymphoma: Involves rearrangements in BCL2, BCL6, and MYC."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Double hit lymphoma: Involves MYC rearrangement along with BCL2 and/or BCL6."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Triple hit lymphoma: Involves rearrangements in BCL2, BCL6, and MYC."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment: First-line treatment: R-CHOP (rituximab, cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisone) is the standard. Early-stage disease: Full-course R-CHOP for six cycles or abbreviated chemotherapy (3-4 cycles) followed by involved field radiotherapy. Advanced-stage disease: Full-course chemotherapy. Relapsed/Refractory Disease: Salvage chemotherapy followed by autologous stem cell transplantation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"First-line treatment: R-CHOP (rituximab, cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisone) is the standard."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early-stage disease: Full-course R-CHOP for six cycles or abbreviated chemotherapy (3-4 cycles) followed by involved field radiotherapy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Advanced-stage disease: Full-course chemotherapy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Relapsed/Refractory Disease: Salvage chemotherapy followed by autologous stem cell transplantation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"First-line treatment: R-CHOP (rituximab, cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisone) is the standard."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early-stage disease: Full-course R-CHOP for six cycles or abbreviated chemotherapy (3-4 cycles) followed by involved field radiotherapy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Advanced-stage disease: Full-course chemotherapy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Relapsed/Refractory Disease: Salvage chemotherapy followed by autologous stem cell transplantation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Follicular Lymphoma (Option A) is a slow-growing B-cell lymphoproliferative disorder that typically presents with painless lymphadenopathy. In contrast, the case involves a rapidly growing abdominal mass with systemic B symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss), and a biopsy showing large atypical cells suggesting a more aggressive disease like diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mantle Cell Lymphoma (Option B) typically follows an aggressive clinical course characterized by overexpression of the cyclin D1 (CCND1) gene. In contrast, diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) lacks Cyclin D1 overexpression and presents with large atypical B cells and rapidly growing tumours."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Classical Hodgkin's Lymphoma (Option C) typically presents in young adults with slow-growing cervical lymphadenopathy and Reed-Sternberg cells in a biopsy, which is inconsistent with the rapidly growing abdominal mass and diffuse large lymphocytes seen in this case are more indicative of diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL)."}]},{"n":6,"q":"All are true regarding marginal zone lymphoma except?","o":[{"t":"It can affect the stomach"},{"t":"It can be associated with Sjögren's syndrome"},{"t":"It originates from small B lymphocytes"},{"t":"R-CHOP regimen is the first-line treatment"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) R-CHOP regimen is the first-line treatment."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The R-CHOP regimen (Rituximab, Cyclophosphamide, Hydroxydaunorubicin, Oncovin, and Prednisone) is not the preferred first-line treatment for MZL, particularly for gastric MALT lymphoma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"For MALT lymphoma, especially in cases associated with H. pylori, the first-line treatment is the eradication of H. pylori with a triple-drug therapy regimen consisting of a proton pump inhibitor (PPI), clarithromycin, and amoxicillin or metronidazole. This approach can lead to the complete remission of gastric MALT lymphoma in many cases."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Marginal Zone Lymphoma / Mucosa-Associated Lymphoid Tissue (MALT) Lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Occurrence:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primarily affects extranodal sites, with the gastrointestinal tract being the most common location; the stomach is particularly affected."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Demographics:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically presents around 60 years of age, with a female predominance."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MALT lymphoma is characterized by the proliferation of small B lymphocytes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Symptoms"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common symptoms include gastrointestinal discomfort, such as nausea and abdominal pain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Approximately 95% of cases are associated with chronic Helicobacter pylori infection, leading to non-atrophic pangastritis. This chronic inflammatory state is a significant risk factor for the development of lymphoma."},{"k":"img","v":6952},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Autoimmune Associations"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MALT lymphoma is associated with several autoimmune disorders, particularly Sjögren's syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other conditions, such as rheumatoid arthritis and Hashimoto's thyroiditis, may also predispose individuals to MALT lymphoma due to chronic inflammation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Immunophenotyping"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Positive Markers: CD20, CD19, CD22"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Negative Markers: CD5, CD10, CD23"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MZL is usually a diagnosis of exclusion if IHC markers are given"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chromosomal Translocation seen in MZL: t( 11:18)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Prognosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"MALT lymphoma generally has a favourable prognosis in the early stages, with 5-year overall survival rates exceeding 90%."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"First-line treatment typically involves an anti-H. pylori regimen, which may include antibiotics (e.g., amoxicillin, clarithromycin) and proton pump inhibitors (PPIs)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In about 5% to 10% of cases, more aggressive treatment, such as the R-CHOP chemotherapy regimen (Rituximab, Cyclophosphamide, Doxorubicin, Vincristine, and Prednisone), may be necessary."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 55-year-old male presents with painless lymphadenopathy. A biopsy of the lymph node reveals the presence of \"buttock cells\". Flow cytometry shows a clonal population of B-cells expressing CD10, CD19, and BCL-2. Based on these findings, what is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Follicular lymphoma"},{"t":"Mantle cell lymphoma"},{"t":"Burkitt lymphoma"},{"t":"Small lymphocytic lymphoma"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Follicular lymphoma"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Follicular lymphoma is a common type of indolent non-Hodgkin lymphoma that arises from germinal centre B-cells . The characteristic histological feature of follicular lymphoma is the presence of \"buttock cells\" (centrocytes), which are cleaved B-cells with irregular, indented nuclei. These cells, along with centroblasts, are key features in the nodular or follicular growth pattern seen in biopsies."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Follicular Lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Follicular lymphoma is the most common type of indolent non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) globally. This malignancy arises from the germinal centre B-cells, specifically from centrocytes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is characterized as a low-grade lymphoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Good prognosis, especially in its early stages."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Epidemiology and Presentation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presents in individuals around 60 years of age and is more prevalent in males."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spontaneous remission can occur in some cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"B-symptoms, such as fever, night sweats, and weight loss, are rare in this condition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extranodal involvement is not common."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In advanced stages, a leukemic phase may be observed."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathogenesis Chromosomal Translocation, t(14;18) fl Juxtaposition of the immunoglobulin heavy chain locus (chromosome 14) and the Bcl-2 gene (chromosome 18). fl Overexpression of Bcl-2 protein (Anti-Apoptotic) fl Inhibition of apoptosis fl Increased survival and proliferation of B cells. fl Development of follicular lymphoma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Immunophenotyping Markers:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Positive Markers: Negative Markers:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"CD19: A pan-B-cell marker. CD5 CD20: Commonly expressed in B-cells. CD23 Bcl-2: (Anti-apoptotic protein)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Histopathology"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lymph Node Architecture:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Displays a follicular pattern with para trabecular lymphoid aggregates."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cells are arranged in follicles, resembling normal lymphoid tissue but with distinct features."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cell Types:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Centrocytes: Small cells with cleaved nuclear contours, sometimes called \"buttock cells.\""},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Centroblasts: Larger cells characterized by prominent nucleoli."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Follicular lymphoma is distinguished from reactive follicular hyperplasia by:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Disruption of normal lymph node architecture which is maintained in follicular hyperplasia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lack of tingible body macrophages in germinal centers which are present in follicular hyperplasia and these macrophages represent apoptosis of Bells"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bcl-2 expression in follicles which is absent in follicular hyperplasia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Monoclonality (follicular hyperplasia is polyclonal)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bone Marrow Examination:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presence of para-trabecular lymphoid aggregates."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Positive for CD19, CD20, and Bcl-2 in these aggregates."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetic Mutations: Approximately 90% of cases may have MLL gene mutations."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Transformation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"30-50% of follicular lymphoma cases may progress to more aggressive forms, such as Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma (DLBCL)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low Tumor Burden: Asymptomatic patients with low tumour burden may have deferred treatment or may be treated with Rituximab alone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High Tumor Burden: Symptomatic patients typically receive combination therapies, such as Bendamustine + Rituximab."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Advanced Disease: Requires an aggressive treatment regimen, such as R-CHOP (Rituximab, Cyclophosphamide, Doxorubicin, Vincristine, and Prednisone)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mantle cell lymphoma (Option B):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CD5+, CD19+, Cyclin D1+ (not CD10+)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Associated with t(11;14) translocation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"More aggressive than follicular lymphoma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CD5+, CD19+, Cyclin D1+ (not CD10+)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Associated with t(11;14) translocation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"More aggressive than follicular lymphoma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Burkitt lymphoma (Option C):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CD10+, CD19+, BCL-2 negative , BCL-6 positive ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"\"Starry sky\" appearance histologically, not buttock cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Associated with c-MYC translocation , usually t(8;14)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CD10+, CD19+, BCL-2 negative , BCL-6 positive ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"\"Starry sky\" appearance histologically, not buttock cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Associated with c-MYC translocation , usually t(8;14)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Small lymphocytic lymphoma (Option D):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CD5+, CD23+, CD19+, CD10 negative ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cells are small, round— not buttock-shaped."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CD5+, CD23+, CD19+, CD10 negative ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cells are small, round— not buttock-shaped."}]},{"n":8,"q":"A patient presents with lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. Biopsy findings reveal a diffuse proliferation of small to medium-sized lymphoid cells with irregular nuclei. Which of the following immunohistochemical (IHC) markers is definitive for diagnosing mantle cell lymphoma?","o":[{"t":"CD5"},{"t":"BCL-2"},{"t":"CD23"},{"t":"Cyclin D1"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Cyclin D1"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mantle cell lymphoma (MCL) is characterized by the overexpression of Cyclin D1, which is a definitive marker for this lymphoma subtype. Cyclin D1 overexpression results from the translocation t(11;14), which is specific to MCL."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other markers like CD5 & BCL-2 are positive but are not exclusive to MCL, as they are also seen in other lymphomas."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CD23 is typically negative in MCL, distinguishing it from CLL/SLL, which usually expresses CD23."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mantle Cell Lymphoma (MCL)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mantle cell lymphoma is a subtype of non-Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by the proliferation of B-lymphocytes that originate from the mantle zone (pre-germinal) of lymphoid follicles."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It is an aggressive lymphoma, often presenting with advanced disease at the time of diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathogenesis:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lymphadenopathy (Enlargement of lymph nodes)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Extranodal Involvement:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gastrointestinal tract (GIT), especially the ileocecal region, where lymphomatoid polyposis may occur."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Waldeyer's Ring Involvement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bone Marrow and Spleen Involvement"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunophenotyping: Utilizing markers such as CD19, CD20, CD5, CD23, and Cyclin D1 . The marker for Cyclin D1-negative mantle cell lymphoma is SOX II."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cytogenetics: t(11;14) translocation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Staging: PET CT scan"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Histopathology: Small irregular lymphocytes with notched nuclei and effacement of lymph node architecture."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"R-CHOP"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rituximab: A monoclonal antibody targeting CD20."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cyclophosphamide"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hydroxy-daunorubicin (Doxorubicin)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vincristine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prednisolone (Corticosteroid)"}]},{"n":9,"q":"Which of the following statements regarding non-Hodgkin lymphoma is incorrect?","o":[{"t":"B-symptoms are mostly seen in advanced disease."},{"t":"Extranodal involvement is very common"},{"t":"It frequently presents at advanced stages (I or II) in the Ann Arbor staging system"},{"t":"B-cell subtypes are more common"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) It frequently presents at advanced stages (I or II) in the Ann Arbor staging system."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma often presents at more advanced stages (III or IV) due to the widespread involvement of lymph nodes and extranodal sites."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lugano classification is also used for staging NHL"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) is a diverse group of hematologic malignancies characterized by the proliferation of lymphoid tissue."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-Hodgkin lymphomas (NHL) into B-cell and T-cell subtypes:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The majority of non-Hodgkin lymphomas are of B-cell origin, making them more prevalent than T-cell subtypes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"B cell NHL is further classified based on cell size, cell pattern, IHC markers and cytogenetic translocations."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Small B cells : Follicular lymphoma Mantle cell lymphoma Marginal zone lymphoma and Small lymphocytic lymphoma (which is CLL-affecting tissues)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Follicular lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mantle cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Marginal zone lymphoma and"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Small lymphocytic lymphoma (which is CLL-affecting tissues)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Follicular lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mantle cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Marginal zone lymphoma and"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Small lymphocytic lymphoma (which is CLL-affecting tissues)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intermediate-size B cells : Burkitt lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Burkitt lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Burkitt lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Large B cells : DLBCL"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DLBCL"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DLBCL"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Based on cell of origin B-NHL Classification:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pre-germinal (Mantle zone) : MZL CLL/SLL"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MZL"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CLL/SLL"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MZL"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CLL/SLL"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Germinal : Follicular cell lymphoma Burkitt lymphoma DLBCL"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Follicular cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Burkitt lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DLBCL"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Follicular cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Burkitt lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DLBCL"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Post germinal : Marginal zone lymphoma/ MALT lymphoma Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Marginal zone lymphoma/ MALT lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Marginal zone lymphoma/ MALT lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma"},{"k":"img","v":6960},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Demographics and Incidence"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NHL is more prevalent in individuals over 60 years of age and demonstrates a slight male predominance."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common Presentations of Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Asymptomatic Palpable Lymphadenopathy:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"One of the hallmark presentations of NHL is unexplained, asymptomatic lymphadenopathy. Patients often notice enlarged lymph nodes, typically in the neck, armpits, or groin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NHL lymphadenopathy is usually not rapidly progressive, which can help differentiate it from other causes of lymph node enlargement."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"B Symptoms (Fever, Night Sweats, and Weight Loss):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These constitutional symptoms, are relatively rare in early-stage NHL but become more significant in advanced disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of B symptoms often indicates a more aggressive or widespread disease process. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Extranodal Involvement"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common Sites:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frequently involves extranodal sites (Option B ruled out)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gastrointestinal tract, particularly the ileocecal region, is commonly affected, leading to symptoms such as abdominal pain or changes in bowel habits."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphomatoid polyposis can also occur."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oropharynx, especially Waldeyer's ring, can be involved, presenting with sore throat or difficulty swallowing."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bone Marrow Involvement:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Significant in NHL, particularly in non-nodal subtypes, bone marrow infiltration can lead to cytopenias (anaemia, leukopenia, and thrombocytopenia)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Splenomegaly:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NHL often results in splenic involvement, which is more pronounced than in Hodgkin lymphoma. This may lead to splenomegaly, causing abdominal discomfort and a sensation of fullness."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Advanced Disease at Diagnosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NHL typically presents at more advanced stages, particularly Stages III and IV according to the Ann Arbor staging system. (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ann Arbor Staging System for Lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Stage Description"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"I Involvement of a single lymph node region"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"II Involvement of two or more lymph node regions on the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"same side of the diaphragm"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"III Involvement of lymph node regions on both sides of the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"diaphragm"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"IV Disseminated disease involving one or more extranodal"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"sites, including bone marrow, liver, or other organs"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Arbor Staging System for Lymphoma\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"10336\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/RTiwSZI6wFy53h 6om1nd1744686133.png\" />"}]},{"n":10,"q":"NF-kB activation is a common event in classical Hodgkin Lymphoma (cHL) and promotes the growth and survival of Reed-Sternberg cells. Which of the following mechanisms is associated with NF-kB activation in EBV-positive tumours?","o":[{"t":"Loss-of-function mutations in IkB"},{"t":"Expression of latent membrane protein-1 (LMP-1)."},{"t":"Activation of the TNF-a–induced protein 3"},{"t":"Decreased levels of cytokine production"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Expression of latent membrane protein-1 (LMP-1)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"LMP-1 is a viral protein in EBV-positive tumours that mimics CD40 , a receptor crucial for B-cell activation. By activating the NF-kB signalling pathway , LMP-1 promotes the survival and proliferation of Reed-Sternberg cells in classical Hodgkin Lymphoma (cHL), facilitating tumour growth and enabling the cells to evade apoptosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Etiology & Pathogenesis of Hodgkin Lymphoma (HL)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hodgkin Lymphoma is a mature B-cell tumour. More common in whites & Males. Bimodal age distribution with peaks in the 20s and 80s. It is not a part of AIDS-defining lymphoma. Risk Factors : HIV infection is a major risk factor. Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Reed-Sternberg (HRS) Cells Etiology & Pathogenesis of Hodgkin Lymphoma (HL)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"HRS cells are derived from the germinal centre or post-germinal centre B cells (mature B cells in peripheral lymph nodes) as revealed by clonal IGH gene rearrangements and somatic hypermutation. Diagnostic of HL. Large, with abundant cytoplasm, bilobed or multinucleated. Typically positive for PAX-5, CD15, and CD30."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"NF-kB Activation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common in cHL, promoting RS cell growth and survival. EBV+ Tumors : Express latent membrane protein-1 (LMP-1) , which activates NF-kB . (Option B) EBV- Tumors : NF-kB activation may result from loss-of-function mutations in IkB or l oss-of-function mutation of TNF-a–induced protein 3 , both of which normally inhibit NF-kB. (Options A & C) NF-kB activation may rescue \"crippled\" B cells that can't express Ig, preventing apoptosis and enabling further mutations that form RS cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tumor Microenvironment"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"RS cells secrete cytokines (e.g., IL-5, IL-10) and chemokines (e.g., eotaxin) that attract reactive cells (e.g., eosinophils, T cells). (Option D) These reactive cells support tumour growth and modify the immune response, with ligands activating RS cell receptors (e.g., CD30, CD40), further upregulating NF-kB."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Immune Evasion"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"97% of HRS cells have genetic abnormalities at the PD-L1 locus on chromosome 9p24.1 , leading to PD-L1 overexpression, which helps the tumour evade immune detection."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Viral Oncogenesis : EBV involvement is stronger in HIV-associated HL, where nearly all cases show EBV expression."}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 45-year-old man presents with rapid-onset weight loss, fatigue, and an enlarging mass in the abdomen. Laboratory tests reveal elevated lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), and a biopsy confirms the presence of a fast-growing malignancy. Immunohistochemical staining shows that the tumour cells are CD19, CD20 and CD79 +ve. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Mycosis fungoides"},{"t":"Burkitt’s lymphoma"},{"t":"Sezary syndrome"},{"t":"Anaplastic large cell lymphoma"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Burkitt’s lymphoma"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Burkitt’s lymphoma is a mature B-cell lymphoma characterized by rapid growth. It typically presents with systemic symptoms such as weight loss, fatigue, and abdominal masses, and it is associated with high LDH levels due to the rapid cell turnover. Immunohistochemical staining showing CD19, CD20, and CD79 positivity is consistent with a B-cell origin, further supporting Burkitt's lymphoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a type of non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-Hodgkin’s lymphomas are malignancies of the mature B, T, or NK cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Different types of lymphomas under these categories have different proliferation rates, with some being indolent, while others are aggressive."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-Hodgkin’s lymphomas are classified based on the cell of origin as follows:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"B-cell origin (90%) T-cell origin (10%)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"These are mature (peripheral) B-cell neoplasms These are mature (peripheral) T-cell neoplasms"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hairy cell leukemia T-cell granular lymphocytic leukemia Waldenstorm macroglobulinemia Adult T-cell lymphoma/leukaemia Follicular lymphoma Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type Mantle cell lymphoma Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma and subtypes Hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma Splenic marginal zone B-cell lymphoma Subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma Extranodal marginal B-cell lymphoma Mycosis fungoides (Option A) High-grade B-cell lymphoma NOS Sezary syndrome (Option C) Burkitt’s lymphoma/Burkitt’s cell leukaemia Peripheral T-cell lymphoma NOS Primary mediastinal large B-cell lymphoma Angioimmunoblastic T-cell lymphoma HHV8+ DCLBL NOS Anaplastic large-cell lymphoma, ALK+ (Option D) Plasmablastic lymphoma Anaplastic large-cell lymphoma, ALK- Intravascular large B-cell leukaemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most prevalent mature B cell neoplasms include: Follicular lymphoma Burkitt lymphoma Diffuse large B cell lymphoma Mantle cell lymphoma Marginal zone lymphoma Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia/ Small lymphocytic lymphoma Primary CNS lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Follicular lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Burkitt lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diffuse large B cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mantle cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Marginal zone lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia/ Small lymphocytic lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary CNS lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Follicular lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Burkitt lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diffuse large B cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mantle cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Marginal zone lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia/ Small lymphocytic lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary CNS lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most common mature T cell lymphomas are: Adult T cell lymphoma Mycosis fungoides"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Adult T cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mycosis fungoides"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Adult T cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mycosis fungoides"}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 45-year-old male is diagnosed with Hodgkin lymphoma. His survival chances are assessed using the International Prognostic Index. Which of the following is not a criterion for this index?","o":[{"t":"Age >45 years"},{"t":"Presence of B symptoms"},{"t":"Serum albumin <4 g/dL"},{"t":"Lymphocyte count <600/mm³"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Presence of B symptoms"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The International Prognostic Index for Hodgkin Lymphoma does not include the presence of B symptoms as a criterion."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"International Prognostic Index of Hodgkin Lymphoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Helps to determine the chances of survival of patients with Hodgkin lymphoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Risk Factors/ Poor Prognostic Factors: Serum albumin <4 g/dL (Option C) Hemoglobin <10.5 g/dL Male gender Ann arbor stage IV disease Age ‡45 years (Option A) White blood cell count > 15,000/mm³ Lymphocyte count <600/mm or <8% of white blood cells (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum albumin <4 g/dL (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemoglobin <10.5 g/dL"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Male gender"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ann arbor stage IV disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Age ‡45 years (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"White blood cell count > 15,000/mm³"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphocyte count <600/mm or <8% of white blood cells (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on these factors, the survival chances of the patient can be assessed."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Predicts 5-year freedom from progression (FFP): 42% to 84%"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"5 year Overall survival (OS): 56% to 89%"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum albumin <4 g/dL (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemoglobin <10.5 g/dL"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Male gender"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ann arbor stage IV disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Age ‡45 years (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"White blood cell count > 15,000/mm³"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphocyte count <600/mm or <8% of white blood cells (Option D)"}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 28-year-old male presents with fever, night sweats, and significant weight loss over the past few months. A lymph node biopsy confirms advanced-stage classical Hodgkin lymphoma. Imaging reveals bulky mediastinal lymphadenopathy. What is the most appropriate treatment for this patient?","o":[{"t":"6 cycles of ABVD regimen alone"},{"t":"Brentuximab combined with nivolumab"},{"t":"4 cycles of ABVD followed by field radiation"},{"t":"Combination of ABVD and R-CHOP regimens"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) 6 cycles of ABVD regimen alone"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Advanced-stage classical Hodgkin lymphoma is commonly managed with 6 cycles of the ABVD regimen (doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, and dacarbazine)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Classical Hodgkin Lymphoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early-Stage Disease: Favourable disease (low-risk): Common regimen: ABVD (doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, and dacarbazine). 4-6 cycles of ABVD alone achieve progression-free survival rates of 88-92%. Unfavorable disease (higher risk): ABVD for 4 cycles + field radiation or 6 cycles of ABVD alone. (Option C) Radiation therapy is more beneficial in a bulky disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Favourable disease (low-risk): Common regimen: ABVD (doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, and dacarbazine). 4-6 cycles of ABVD alone achieve progression-free survival rates of 88-92%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common regimen: ABVD (doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, and dacarbazine)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"4-6 cycles of ABVD alone achieve progression-free survival rates of 88-92%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Unfavorable disease (higher risk): ABVD for 4 cycles + field radiation or 6 cycles of ABVD alone. (Option C) Radiation therapy is more beneficial in a bulky disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ABVD for 4 cycles + field radiation or 6 cycles of ABVD alone. (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Radiation therapy is more beneficial in a bulky disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Favourable disease (low-risk): Common regimen: ABVD (doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, and dacarbazine). 4-6 cycles of ABVD alone achieve progression-free survival rates of 88-92%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common regimen: ABVD (doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, and dacarbazine)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"4-6 cycles of ABVD alone achieve progression-free survival rates of 88-92%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Unfavorable disease (higher risk): ABVD for 4 cycles + field radiation or 6 cycles of ABVD alone. (Option C) Radiation therapy is more beneficial in a bulky disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ABVD for 4 cycles + field radiation or 6 cycles of ABVD alone. (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Radiation therapy is more beneficial in a bulky disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common regimen: ABVD (doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, and dacarbazine)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"4-6 cycles of ABVD alone achieve progression-free survival rates of 88-92%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ABVD for 4 cycles + field radiation or 6 cycles of ABVD alone. (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Radiation therapy is more beneficial in a bulky disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Advanced-Stage Disease: Treated primarily with 6 cycles of ABVD alone. (Option A) Alternative Chemotherapy Regimens: Stanford V regimen BEACOPP regimen (bleomycin, etoposide, doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide, vincristine, procarbazine and prednisolone)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treated primarily with 6 cycles of ABVD alone. (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alternative Chemotherapy Regimens: Stanford V regimen BEACOPP regimen (bleomycin, etoposide, doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide, vincristine, procarbazine and prednisolone)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stanford V regimen"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"BEACOPP regimen (bleomycin, etoposide, doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide, vincristine, procarbazine and prednisolone)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treated primarily with 6 cycles of ABVD alone. (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alternative Chemotherapy Regimens: Stanford V regimen BEACOPP regimen (bleomycin, etoposide, doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide, vincristine, procarbazine and prednisolone)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stanford V regimen"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"BEACOPP regimen (bleomycin, etoposide, doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide, vincristine, procarbazine and prednisolone)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stanford V regimen"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"BEACOPP regimen (bleomycin, etoposide, doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide, vincristine, procarbazine and prednisolone)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Relapsed Disease: Standard salvage chemotherapy followed by autologous stem cell transplantation. Standard salvage chemotherapy regimens include ICE (ifosfamide, carboplatin and etoposide) and GND (gemcitabine, vinorelbine and doxorubicin) Newer combinations: Brentuximab with either chemotherapy or immune checkpoint inhibitors (nivolumab, pembrolizumab). (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Standard salvage chemotherapy followed by autologous stem cell transplantation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Standard salvage chemotherapy regimens include ICE (ifosfamide, carboplatin and etoposide) and GND (gemcitabine, vinorelbine and doxorubicin)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Newer combinations: Brentuximab with either chemotherapy or immune checkpoint inhibitors (nivolumab, pembrolizumab). (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Standard salvage chemotherapy followed by autologous stem cell transplantation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Standard salvage chemotherapy regimens include ICE (ifosfamide, carboplatin and etoposide) and GND (gemcitabine, vinorelbine and doxorubicin)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Newer combinations: Brentuximab with either chemotherapy or immune checkpoint inhibitors (nivolumab, pembrolizumab). (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin's Lymphoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early-Stage: Definitive radiotherapy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptomatic patients or those with organ impairment: Treated with both classical HL regimens (ABVD) and B-cell NHL regimens (R-CHOP). (Option D)"}]},{"n":14,"q":"A 45-year-old male presents with painless cervical lymphadenopathy with no extranodal involvement. Immunohistochemistry reveals cells positive for CD20, CD19, and CD45 but negative for CD30 and CD15. He has a history of chronic, relapsing symptoms. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Follicular Lymphoma"},{"t":"Diffuse Large B-cell lymphoma"},{"t":"Classical Hodgkin's Lymphoma"},{"t":"Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin's Lymphoma"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin's Lymphoma"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Regional lymphadenopathy with no extranodal involvement, chronic relapsing symptoms, and positive immunohistochemistry for CD20, CD19, and CD45 point towards a diagnosis of Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin's Lymphoma (NLPHL)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"WHO Modified Revised European-American Lymphoma (REAL) Classification:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nodular Lymphocyte Predominant Hodgkin Lymphoma (5%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Classical Hodgkin Lymphoma Nodular Sclerosing (70%) Mixed Cellularity (20%) Lymphocyte-Rich (5%) Lymphocyte-Depleted (rare)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nodular Sclerosing (70%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mixed Cellularity (20%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphocyte-Rich (5%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphocyte-Depleted (rare)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nodular Sclerosing (70%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mixed Cellularity (20%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphocyte-Rich (5%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphocyte-Depleted (rare)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin's Lymphoma (NLPHL): (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Distinct from classical Hodgkin’s lymphoma (cHL)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Accounts for < 5% of all Hodgkin's lymphoma cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Shows a nodular growth pattern."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Predominance of small lymphocytes and rare Reed-Sternberg-like cells or L&H; (lymphocyte and histiocyte) or \"popcorn\" cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"L&H; cells are a clonal proliferation of B-cells positive for CD20, CD19, CD45, CD79a, BCL2, and BCL6."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Negative for CD30 and CD15, which are typically found on classical Hodgkin Reed-Sternberg (HRS) cells. (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic relapsing courses are common."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"May transform to diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) , especially the T-cell/histiocyte-rich subtype."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"More common in males (75% of cases)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment: Early-Stage: Definitive radiotherapy Symptomatic patients or those with organ impairment: Treated with both classical HL regimens (ABVD) and B-cell NHL regimens (R-CHOP)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early-Stage: Definitive radiotherapy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptomatic patients or those with organ impairment: Treated with both classical HL regimens (ABVD) and B-cell NHL regimens (R-CHOP)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early-Stage: Definitive radiotherapy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptomatic patients or those with organ impairment: Treated with both classical HL regimens (ABVD) and B-cell NHL regimens (R-CHOP)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Follicular Lymphoma (Option A) typically presents with generalized painless lymphadenopathy that is waxing and waning, often involving multiple lymph node regions and extranodal sites. This contrasts with the localized lymphadenopathy and chronic relapsing symptoms seen in Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin's Lymphoma (NLPHL)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diffuse Large B-cell lymphoma (Option B) typically presents as a rapidly growing mass with extranodal involvement and does not align with the chronic relapsing symptoms and localized lymphadenopathy characteristic of Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin's Lymphoma (NLPHL)."}]},{"n":15,"q":"A 35-year-old male presents with unexplained fever, night sweats, and a 12% weight loss over 6 months. A physical exam reveals palpable lymph nodes in the neck and axilla. PET/CT shows lymph node involvement on both sides of the diaphragm and splenic enlargement without extranodal disease. Based on the Ann Arbor Staging System, what is the correct stage for this patient’s disease?","o":[{"t":"Stage IIB"},{"t":"Stage III1B"},{"t":"Stage III2A"},{"t":"Stage IVB"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Stage III1B"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient has the involvement o f lymph nodes on both sides of the diaphragm , which indicates stage III disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of systemic symptoms (fever, night sweats, and weight loss) classifies it as \" B\" symptoms ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Since the spleen is involved, but there is no mention of extranodal involvement beyond the spleen and lymph nodes, stage IV is not applicable. Therefore, the correct classification is Stage III1B ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Modified Ann Arbor Classification"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Stage Definition"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"I Involvement of a single lymph node region or lymphoid"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"structure (e.g., spleen, thymus, Waldeyer’s ring)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"II Involvement of 2 or more lymph node regions on the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"same side of the diaphragm. Mediastinum counts as one site; hilar lymph nodes are considered \"lateralized,\" and if both sides are involved, it is stage II disease. (Option A)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"III Involvement of lymph node regions or lymphoid"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"structures on both sides of the diaphragm."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"III1 Subdiaphragmatic involvement is limited to the spleen, splenic hilar nodes, celiac nodes, or portal nodes. (Option B) III2 Subdiaphragmatic involvement includes paraaortic, iliac, or mesenteric nodes plus structures from III1. (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"IV Involvement of extranodal sites beyond “E” designation,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"more than one extranodal deposit at any location, or involvement of the liver or bone marrow. (Option D)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A - No systemic symptoms. B - Unexplained weight loss >10% in 6 months, persistent or recurrent fever >38°C, or recurrent night sweats in the past months. E - Localized involvement of extra lymphatic tissue, excluding liver and bone marrow."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Arbor Classification\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"10334\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/AuKBYKOsSBKnEuHa7BfE1744 685463.png\" />"}]},{"n":16,"q":"A 45-year-old female presents with cervical lymphadenopathy and constitutional symptoms, including unexplained weight loss and night sweats. Imaging studies suggest the presence of a mediastinal mass. An excisional biopsy of a cervical lymph node is given below. Which of the following findings is most likely to be present in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Positive for CD15 and CD30"},{"t":"High expression of CD20 and BCL6"},{"t":"Negative for PAX-5"},{"t":"Positive for CD19"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Positive for CD15 and CD30"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Excisional biopsy of a cervical lymph node shows classical Reed-Sternberg cells typically exhibit low or absent expression of B-cell markers like CD19 , consistent with classical Hodgkin lymphoma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Workup of Hodgkin's Lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Comprehensive history and physical examination , with attention to lymph nodes, liver, spleen, and B symptoms. Chest radiograph if there is a mediastinal mass to evaluate for bulky disease. Imaging : PET/CT scan (imaging modality of choice) for staging , which is more accurate than bone marrow biopsy due to patchy bone marrow involvement in cHL. Complete blood count (CBC) , ESR . Liver function tests (alkaline phosphatase, lactate dehydrogenase , albumin, bilirubin). Kidney function tests : BUN, creatinine. Thyroid function tests if neck radiotherapy is considered. Pregnancy test for women of childbearing age. Echocardiogram or radionuclide study for ejection fraction if anthracycline chemotherapy is planned. Pulmonary function tests with diffusing capacity if bleomycin-containing chemotherapy is to be used. Infection panel for HIV, Hepatitis, EBV."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Workup of Hodgkin's Lymphoma"},{"k":"img","v":6911},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigation of choice: Excision lymph node biopsy, which shows Reed-Sternberg (HRS) Cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"HRS cells are large with abundant cytoplasm and bilobed or multinucleated nuclei (Owleye appearance), making them characteristic of classical Hodgkin lymphoma (cHL). Flow cytometry: CD15 and CD30 +ve in classical Hodgkin lymphoma . (Option A) L&H; (NLPHL) variants show CD20 and BCL6 +ve (Option B) and are usually negative for CD15 and CD30. Low or absent expression of B-cell markers CD19 (Option D) and CD20 . Positive for PAX-5. (Option C)"}],"qi":[6911]},{"n":17,"q":"A 28-year-old male presents with jaundice, pruritus, and fatigue. He reports experiencing night sweats, fever, and unintentional weight loss over the past month. Laboratory tests reveal elevated alkaline phosphatase and bilirubin levels. A liver biopsy shows loss of intrahepatic bile ducts. Further evaluation shows enlarged lymph nodes in the cervical region. What is the most likely underlying diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Primary biliary cholangitis"},{"t":"Classical Hodgkin Lymphoma"},{"t":"Hepatitis C infection"},{"t":"Alcoholic liver disease"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Classical Hodgkin Lymphoma"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The combination of vanishing bile duct syndrome (loss of intrahepatic bile ducts) and cervical lymphadenopathy is suggestive of an underlying malignancy. The patient’s B symptoms (night sweats, fever, and unintentional weight loss) are classic indicators of Classical Hodgkin Lymphoma , which can lead to autoimmune liver complications."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Paraneoplastic Findings in Classical Hodgkin Lymphoma (cHL)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Rare paraneoplastic syndromes have been observed at the time of cHL diagnosis. Syndromes include: Vanishing bile duct syndrome (Option B) and idiopathic cholangitis with clinical jaundice. Nephrotic syndrome (membranous glomerulopathy) with anasarca. Autoimmune hematologic disorders such as immune thrombocytopenia or hemolytic anaemia. Various neurologic signs and symptoms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"While direct CNS or meningeal involvement in cHL is rare, neurological paraneoplastic syndromes include: Subacute cerebellar degeneration . Myelopathy. Progressive multifocal encephalopathy. Limbic encephalitis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypercalcemia"}]},{"n":18,"q":"A 25-year-old male presents with a 3-month history of painless, enlarged cervical lymph nodes that are nontender and \"rubbery\" in consistency. He reports fever, night sweats, and unintentional weight loss of about 10% over the last six months. Additionally, he experiences pain in the lymph nodes after consuming alcohol. There are no complaints of cough or dyspnea, and his complete blood count is unremarkable. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Non-Hodgkin lymphoma"},{"t":"Hodgkin lymphoma"},{"t":"Tuberculosis"},{"t":"Sarcoidosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Hodgkin lymphoma"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient exhibits classical B symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss) and nontender, rubbery cervical lymphadenopathy , both characteristic of Hodgkin lymphoma . The presence of pain in lymph nodes after alcohol consumption is a highly specific symptom of this condition."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical presentation of Hodgkin lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lymphadenopathy (Swollen Lymph Nodes)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Most common presentation: ~60-70% in cervical/supraclavicular nodes, 15-20% in axillary nodes. Non-tender , \"rubbery\" lymph nodes. Over half have mediastinal adenopathy, sometimes the first sign. Subdiaphragmatic involvement is rare and more common in older males. Pain in lymph nodes after alcohol consumption . Seen in <10% of patients but highly specific to cHL ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"B Symptoms (Constitutional Symptoms)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Present in ~30% of cases. Includes fever >38°C, night sweats, and unexplained weight loss (>10% in 6 months). Fever is often low-grade and irregular. Rarely, Pel-Ebstein fever : cyclic high fevers (1-2 weeks) alternating with afebrile periods."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical presentation of Hodgkin lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Respiratory Symptoms (Intrathoracic Disease)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cough, chest pain, difficulty breathing (dyspnea), and rarely, coughing up blood (hemoptysis)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bone Pain"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Occasional, sometimes with spinal cord compression symptoms like back pain."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Other Symptoms"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Superior vena cava compression : facial swelling, neck vein engorgement. Rare signs: pleural effusion, hepatosplenomegaly, or intra-abdominal masses. Generalized itching , sometimes severe with excoriation and skin disorders (e.g., erythema nodosum, ichthyosiform atrophy)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (Option A) can cause lymphadenopathy and systemic symptoms; it does not typically present with alcohol-induced lymph node pain and may involve multiple extranodal sites more aggressively."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tuberculosis (Option C) usually presents with tender lymph nodes and respiratory symptoms, such as cough. The absence of respiratory issues and the specific symptoms of alcohol-related pain make TB less likely."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sarcoidosis (Option D) commonly features bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy & respiratory symptoms, with lymph nodes that are often tender. Alcohol-induced lymph node pain is not characteristic of sarcoidosis."}]}]);
QB.imgs({"6952":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6924":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6960":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6929":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6900":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6934":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6941":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6911":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});